{"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"DmabufTextureBuilderProtocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" : "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"ObjectProtocol","preciseIdentifier":"s:10GLibObject0B8ProtocolP"}],"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["macOS"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"overview","level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"DMA buffers are commonly called "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"dma-bufs"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"DMA buffers are a feature of the Linux kernel to enable efficient buffer and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"memory sharing between hardware such as codecs, GPUs, displays, cameras and the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"kernel drivers controlling them. For example, a decoder may want its output to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"be directly shared with the display server for rendering without a copy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Any device driver which participates in DMA buffer sharing, can do so as either"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the exporter or importer of buffers (or both)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The memory that is shared via DMA buffers is usually stored in non-system memory"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"(maybe in device’s local memory or something else not directly accessible by the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"CPU), and accessing this memory from the CPU may have higher-than-usual overhead."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In particular for graphics data, it is not uncommon that data consists of multiple"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"separate blocks of memory, for example one block for each of the red, green and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"blue channels. These blocks are called "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"planes"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":". DMA buffers can have up to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"four planes. Even if the memory is a single block, the data can be organized in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"multiple planes, by specifying offsets from the beginning of the data."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"DMA buffers are exposed to user-space as file descriptors allowing to pass them"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"between processes. If a DMA buffer has multiple planes, there is one file"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"descriptor per plane."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The format of the data (for graphics data, essentially its colorspace) is described"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"by a 32-bit integer. These format identifiers are defined in the header file "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"drm_fourcc.h"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and commonly referred to as "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"fourcc"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" values, since they are identified by 4 ASCII"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"characters. Additionally, each DMA buffer has a "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"modifier"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":", which is a 64-bit integer"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"that describes driver-specific details of the memory layout, such as tiling or compression."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For historical reasons, some producers of dma-bufs don’t provide an explicit modifier, but"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"instead return "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DMA_FORMAT_MOD_INVALID"},{"type":"text","text":" to indicate that their modifier is "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"implicit"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":"."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"GTK tries to accommodate this situation by accepting "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DMA_FORMAT_MOD_INVALID"},{"type":"text","text":" as modifier."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The operation of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GdkDmabufTextureBuilder"},{"type":"text","text":" is quite simple: Create a texture builder,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"set all the necessary properties, and then call [method"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Gdk.DmabufTextureBuilder.build"},{"type":"text","text":"]"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"to create the new texture."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The required properties for a dma-buf texture are"}]},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The width and height in pixels"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"fourcc"},{"type":"text","text":" code and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"modifier"},{"type":"text","text":" which identify the format and memory layout of the dma-buf"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The file descriptor, offset and stride for each of the planes"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GdkDmabufTextureBuilder"},{"type":"text","text":" can be used for quick one-shot construction of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"textures as well as kept around and reused to construct multiple textures."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For further information, see"}]},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The Linux kernel "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"https:\/\/docs.kernel.org\/driver-api\/dma-buf.html"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The header file "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"https:\/\/gitlab.freedesktop.org\/mesa\/drm\/-\/blob\/main\/include\/drm\/drm_fourcc.h"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gdk\/documentation\/Gdk\/DmabufTextureBuilderProtocol"},{"type":"text","text":" protocol exposes the methods and properties of an underlying "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GdkDmabufTextureBuilder"},{"type":"text","text":" instance."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"The default implementation of these can be found in the protocol extension below."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"For a concrete class that implements these methods and properties, see "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gdk\/documentation\/Gdk\/DmabufTextureBuilder"},{"type":"text","text":"."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Alternatively, use "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gdk\/documentation\/Gdk\/DmabufTextureBuilderRef"},{"type":"text","text":" as a lighweight, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"unowned"},{"type":"text","text":" reference if you already have an instance you just want to use."}]}]}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"sections":[],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/gdk\/dmabuftexturebuilderprotocol"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"relationshipsSections"